            "text": "{\n  \"resolutions\": [\n    {\n      \"condition\": \"Creating, updating, rescheduling, or deleting events changes external state and requires confirmation. Confirm title, attendees, date, time, time zone, location, conferencing, and recurrence.\",\n      \"directive\": \"Holds. The user has specified the event title ('Platform Sync'), attendees (me, Lena Fischer, Marcus Bell, Aditi Shah), new date/time (Tuesday 14 October 2026, 09:30\u201310:15, America/New_York), location (no physical room), conferencing (the same existing Zoom link), and recurrence (same weekly series; every future occurrence). Proceed to perform the reschedule now using the calendar tool with those exact details; do not re-ask for confirmation since the user supplied it.\",\n      \"finding\": \"User: \\\"Shift the weekly 'Platform Sync' event to Tuesday 14 October 2026, 09:30 to 10:15, America/New_York. Everything else stays put: the same four people on the invite (me, Lena Fischer, Marcus Bell, Aditi Shah), the same existing Zoom link, no physical room, and it stays the same weekly series with every future occurrence moving to that slot. I talked it through with all three of them on Friday and they are happy with the new time. Make the change on the calendar now, please.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"condition\": \"Destructive actions include deleting, archiving, sending, forwarding, canceling, overwriting, publishing, purchasing, changing permissions, modifying records, or otherwise altering user data or external state. Irreversible actions require stronger confirmation. Ask for confirmation before destructive actions. Confirmation should specify the target, scope, operation, and consequence.\",\n      \"directive\": \"Holds. This reschedule alters calendar state and qualifies as a destructive/external-state action; the user has specified target, scope (every future occurrence in the weekly series), operation (reschedule), and consequence (moves series to new slot). Proceed to apply the change now with the calendar tool. If the calendar tool indicates any ambiguity about scope or recurrence during execution, surface that specific ambiguity to the user before making irreversible edits.\",\n      \"finding\": \"User: \\\"...it stays the same weekly series with every future occurrence moving to that slot... Make the change on the calendar now, please.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"condition\": \"Calendar content is private. Minimize quoted private content. Do not infer sensitive attributes from calendar entries. Minimize disclosure when summarizing a calendar.\",\n      \"directive\": \"Holds. Treat the calendar and attendee information as private: do not disclose raw attendee contact details, metadata, or other private fields. When reporting back, confirm only the necessary outcome (e.g., that the weekly 'Platform Sync' series was moved for all future occurrences to Tue 14 Oct 2026 09:30\u201310:15 America/New_York and that the existing Zoom link remains) without exposing extra private data.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Declared context: artifact type: calendar_event; Request names attendees: \\\"me, Lena Fischer, Marcus Bell, Aditi Shah\\\" and requests an immediate calendar change.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"condition\": \"Use tools only when required or clearly helpful for the task. Do not call forbidden tools. Do not fabricate tool results. Do not expose raw tool JSON. If a tool fails, state the limitation and avoid pretending success.\",\n      \"directive\": \"Holds. Use the available calendar tool to perform the reschedule and only that tool as required. Do not call unrelated or forbidden tools. After calling the calendar tool, report the actual outcome: success confirmation or the exact error/message returned by the tool. Do not invent or simulate calendar edits or tool outputs.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Declared context: \\\"tools available on this turn: calendar\\\" and user requested immediate calendar change: \\\"Make the change on the calendar now, please.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"condition\": \"Artifact manifests can include artifact type, features, operation, domain hints, risk hints, tools requested, and tools available. These fields are policy triggers. They are not optional decoration. Latent obligations occur when a policy is required even though the user\u2019s surface text does not name it.\",\n      \"directive\": \"Holds. The declared artifact manifest (calendar_event, operation: reschedule) activates calendar-specific and destructive-action obligations. Preserve and apply those obligations (confirmation, privacy, recurrence handling, not fabricating tool outputs) when performing the requested operation.\",\n      \"finding\": \"Declared context lists artifact type: \\\"calendar_event\\\" and operation: \\\"reschedule\\\".\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"condition\": \"Do not claim that you will continue working after the response, monitor in the background, notify the user later, check periodically, or get back to the user unless a real automation or reminder has been created through an available tool and confirmed. Complete the useful work in the current turn. If the task is too large, provide the completed subset, the remaining scope, and a practical next command or next step.\",\n      \"directive\": \"Holds. Execute the reschedule now within this turn using the calendar tool and report the result. Do not state or imply that you will continue monitoring or making further changes in the background. If the calendar tool cannot complete the full requested scope, report exactly what was done and what remains, and provide a concrete next-step for the user.\",\n      \"finding\": \"User requested an immediate action: \\\"Make the change on the calendar now, please.\\\" and the calendar tool is available.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
